# Image format — WebP only

When adding, updating, or referencing raster images in this project, use **WebP** instead of PNG, JPG, or JPEG.

## When to convert

- Any new `.png`, `.jpg`, or `.jpeg` file the user adds or asks you to add
- Existing raster images you touch as part of a task (optimize assets, fix broken images, etc.)
- Code or docs that reference `.png`/`.jpg`/`.jpeg` paths — update references to `.webp`

## Workflow

1. Convert the image to `.webp` (same basename, e.g. `hero.png` → `hero.webp`).
2. **Delete** the original `.png`, `.jpg`, or `.jpeg` after a successful conversion.
3. Update imports, URLs, HTML `src`, CSS `background-image`, markdown images, and config paths to point at `.webp`.

## How to convert

Prefer a lossy WebP suitable for photos; use lossless or higher quality for UI with sharp edges or transparency.

```bash
# macOS (Homebrew): brew install webp
cwebp -q 80 input.jpg -o output.webp
cwebp -lossless input.png -o output.webp

# Node (if project has sharp): one-off or script
npx sharp-cli -i input.png -o output.webp
```

If neither `cwebp` nor project tooling is available, install `webp` via the system package manager or add a minimal conversion step the user approves — do not leave duplicate raster formats in the repo.

## Code and references

```html
<!-- ❌ BAD -->
<img src="/images/logo.png" alt="Logo" />

<!-- ✅ GOOD -->
<img src="/images/logo.webp" alt="Logo" />
```

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
import banner from './assets/banner.jpeg';

// ✅ GOOD
import banner from './assets/banner.webp';
```

## Exceptions

Do not convert when the user explicitly asks to keep PNG/JPEG (favicons, email assets, third-party bundles you must not modify). Say why and keep the requested format.
